    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Adyghe peoples</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, the Kuban river and the north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>.</li><li>It had no king or capital: princely societies and self-governing free communities shared a language and the code <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adyghe-khabze">Adyghe Khabze</a>.</li><li>A century of war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> ended in 1864 with conquest and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>From the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barquq">Barquq</a> in 1382 until 1517, most sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> who had been brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> as slave soldiers.</p></aside>
    </section>

