    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/timur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/timur.jpg" alt="Timur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Timur_reconstruction03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">shakko, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Circassia was the country of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Adyghe</a>, or Circassian, peoples, who lived between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast, the Kuban river and the foothills of the north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. Their ancestors appear in Greek and Roman sources as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zygii">Zygoi</a> or Zichoi, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the coast was known as Zichia, a land of Byzantine Christian influence where Genoese merchants later kept trading posts such as Matrega and Copa. The Mongol conquests of the thirteenth century and the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> at the end of the fourteenth devastated the region, and the start date of 1400 is a convention marking the period when a recognisable Circassia, a country of related principalities and tribal communities, took shape in the aftermath. It never had a founding king or a single capital. Tradition attributes the rise of Kabarda, the most powerful eastern Circassian principality, to the fifteenth-century prince Inal, regarded as the ancestor of the Kabardian princely families, but his life is known mainly from legend. By the sixteenth century Circassians occupied a broad territory from the Taman peninsula in the west to Kabarda on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terek-river">Terek</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malka-river">Malka</a> rivers in the east, and were known across the Black Sea world as warriors, horse breeders and, less happily, as a major source of slaves.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/black-sea"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-sea.jpg" alt="Black Sea" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Danube_Spills_into_the_Black_Sea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The SeaWiFS Project, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Circassian society was organised by rank and by kinship. In the princely societies, such as Kabarda, Temirgoy, Bzhedugh, Hatuqwai and Besleney, hereditary princes, the <em>pshi</em>, stood above a class of nobles, the <em>work</em>, who served them as warriors and counsellors, and beneath these lived free peasants, dependent peasants and slaves. Among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapsugs">Shapsugs</a>, Natukhaj and Abadzekhs of the west, the power of princes and nobles was weaker and largely broken in the late eighteenth century, and free communities governed themselves. A strict unwritten code of conduct, <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adyghe-khabze">Adyghe Khabze</a></em>, regulated hospitality, honour, respect for elders, and relations between ranks and between men and women. Noble children were often raised by foster families of lower rank, a custom known as <em>atalyk</em> that bound families together. The economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and other grains, orchards, beekeeping and above all livestock, and Circassian horses, such as the Kabardian breed, were famous. Trade ran mainly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> ports, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> exchanged hides, honey, wax, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and slaves for salt, textiles and metal goods. Religion changed over the centuries: older beliefs mixed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> spread from the sixteenth to the early nineteenth century through Crimean and Ottoman influence, first among the nobility and then more widely.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aleksey-yermolov"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aleksey-yermolov.jpg" alt="Aleksey Yermolov" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aleksey-yermolov">Aleksey Yermolov</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexei-jermolov.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Dawe, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Circassian warfare centred on the mounted warrior, armed with sabre, bow and later firearms, protected by mail shirts and helmets, and trained from childhood in horsemanship. The long sabre that Russians called the <em>shashka</em> and the Circassian coat with cartridge pockets, the <em>cherkeska</em>, were later adopted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> and spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. Circassian princes fought the Crimean Khanate, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogais">Nogais</a> and one another, and Kabarda allied with Muscovy in the 1550s; in 1708 Kabardian forces defeated a Crimean army, reportedly much larger, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kanzhal">Battle of Kanzhal</a>, ending Crimean claims to tribute from Kabarda. The great conflict was with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, whose fortified lines advanced along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terek-river">Terek</a> and Kuban from the 1760s, beginning with the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozdok">Mozdok</a> in 1763. Kabarda resisted in repeated wars but was subdued by about 1822 under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleksey-yermolov">Aleksey Yermolov</a>. In the west the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapsugs">Shapsugs</a>, Natukhaj and Abadzekhs waged a long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war in the forests and mountains, raiding Cossack settlements and, in 1840, storming several Russian forts on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast. After 1859, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam-shamil">Imam Shamil</a> surrendered in the eastern Caucasus, Russia concentrated large forces in the west; systematic campaigns burned villages and drove the population towards the coast until resistance ended in May 1864.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/imam-shamil"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imam-shamil.jpg" alt="Imam Shamil" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/imam-shamil">Imam Shamil</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shamil_by_Denier_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andrey Denyer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Circassia was never a single state. It was a cultural and political community of a dozen or so principalities and self-governing tribal societies, each with its own leaders, and its unity rested on a shared language, customs and law rather than on a ruler or a capital. In the princely societies the princes judged disputes, led war parties and represented their people to neighbouring powers, but they ruled together with nobles and needed the consent of assemblies for major decisions. In Kabarda the senior prince, chosen by seniority from the princely families, presided over a council of princes and nobles. In the western societies after the late eighteenth century, when free communities rose against their nobles in a struggle that culminated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bziyuk">Battle of Bziyuk</a> in 1796, power passed to assemblies of elders and representatives that could make war and peace. Customary law, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adat">adat</a></em>, was applied by mediators and courts of respected men, while Islamic law, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a></em>, gained ground with the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Facing Russian conquest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> tried to create common institutions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-amin-naib-of-shamil">Muhammad Amin</a>, a representative of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam-shamil">Imam Shamil</a>, organised courts and taxes among the Abadzekhs in the 1850s, and in 1861 delegates met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sochi">Sochi</a> in a united assembly, often called the Circassian parliament, which appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> for support.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/moscow"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moscow.jpg" alt="Moscow" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Basil%27s_Cathedral_and_the_Red_Square.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. Department of State, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> shaped the history of lands far beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. Enslaved Circassian boys bought for the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> formed the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burji-mamluks">Burji Mamluks</a>, and from the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barquq">Barquq</a> in 1382 until the Ottoman conquest of 1517 most sultans of Egypt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> were Circassians. Circassian women were prized in Ottoman and other Muslim households, and men of Circassian origin rose to high office in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and in Egypt. Kabarda’s alliance with Muscovy brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-temryukovna">Maria Temryukovna</a>, daughter of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temryuk-idarov">Temryuk of Kabarda</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> as the second wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan the Terrible</a> in 1561, and Kabardian princely families entered the Russian nobility. Circassian dress, weapons and horsemanship influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> and the wider military culture of the Caucasus. Western travellers and writers, from the Genoese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgio-interiano">Giorgio Interiano</a> around 1500 to the Briton James Bell in the 1830s, described Circassian society, and the Circassian war became a cause in British politics after the Russian seizure of the British schooner <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vixen-affair">Vixen</a></em> in 1836. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shora-nogmov">Shora Nogmov</a>, a Kabardian scholar, compiled a grammar and a history of the Adyghe in the 1830s and 1840s. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> of 1864 created a large Circassian diaspora that lives today in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, Jordan, Syria, Israel and other countries, and its memory remains central to Circassian identity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crimean-war.jpg" alt="Crimean War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Simpson_-_Attack_on_the_Malakoff.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">William Simpson, artist (1823-1899); E[dmond]. Morin, lithographer (1824 - 1882), working for &quot;Day &amp; Son, lithrs. to the Queen&quot; …, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The end of Circassia came through a century of Russian expansion. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-belgrade">Treaty of Belgrade</a> of 1739 declared Kabarda a neutral barrier between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, but by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kucuk-kaynarca">Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca</a> of 1774 the Ottomans recognised Kabarda as Russian, and in the early nineteenth century Russian forts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> settlements cut deep into its lands. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-adrianople-1829">Treaty of Adrianople</a> of 1829 the Ottoman Empire ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast of Circassia to Russia, a transfer the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> did not accept because they had never been Ottoman subjects. The western Circassians resisted for three more decades, encouraged by hopes of British and Ottoman help that never materialised in decisive form, even during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>. After 1859 the Russian command under Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-yevdokimov">Nikolai Yevdokimov</a> adopted a policy of clearing the highlands: villages were destroyed and Circassians were offered resettlement on the Kuban plain or departure to the Ottoman Empire. Resistance ended in May 1864, when Russian forces held a victory parade at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krasnaya-polyana">Kbaada</a>, now Krasnaya Polyana. Hundreds of thousands of Circassians were deported or fled across the Black Sea, and many died of hunger and disease on the coast, at sea or in Ottoman camps; estimates of the numbers vary widely. Many Circassians call these events a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> recognised them as such in 2011. Small Circassian communities remained in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> under Russian rule.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Circassia was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Adyghe peoples</a>, a stateless country of princes and free communities that supplied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultans, resisted Russian expansion for a century, and was destroyed in 1864 by conquest and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportation</a>, creating a diaspora that remains across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>.</em></p>

